														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3599586">
									

<p>I noticed that I had downvoted this, which must have happened while you were in the middle of posting it - I removed it now that the rest of this is here.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;I&nbsp;like&nbsp;the&nbsp;idea&nbsp;a&nbsp;lot,&nbsp;though...</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>&#8230;there's something I can't quite put my finger on that's preventing me from upvoting (so far; I change my mind a lot). We've had a few stories before about people who can't register in the awareness of other people, but the parental rejection angle and this basically being a kid rather than a trained agent or anything makes it stand out enough that it doesn't suffer from those comparisons IMO.</p>
<p>I think maybe there's just something missing &#8212; the implications that this character has something big planned, with no hints at all as to what that might be (that I caught) make it feel incomplete. This also so unstructured it feels more like a tale, which is no bad thing, but may be helping to throw me off. Some more traces of the &quot;original&quot; containment procedures being left in would both give background into what this ghost-person has been through so far and help it feel more like a proper SCP. [Edit: Read it again and got that this person was never contained, they've put themselves in because they're desperate to be noticed. That's neat.]</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Either way, at its core this is pretty cool, and I'm excited to see where you're going with it.</p>

							</div>

								
